.DS_Store
target/
node_modules/
.env
.env.*

# `/plan`, not `plan/`. In the three development worktrees `plan` is a *symlink*
# to the shared plan repository, and a pattern ending in `/` matches directories
# only — so `plan/` left the symlink untracked-but-committable and `git add -A`
# would have put it on a code branch. DEV-PROTOCOL §9 forbids exactly that.
/plan

# Python bytecode from the differential reference. `reference/` is source that two
# implementations are diffed against; a .pyc in a commit is noise at best and, in a
# layer whose whole value is that its arithmetic was derived rather than ported, a
# stale one is worse than noise.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Tool output that must never reach a commit
.stellar/
mutants.out*/
fuzz/target/
fuzz/corpus/
fuzz/artifacts/

# TypeScript build output. `packages/common` compiles to `dist/` so that Node can import it from
# `node_modules` — type-stripping does not apply under `node_modules`, so a built artifact is the
# only thing a workspace consumer can require.
#
# It is ignored for a second reason that is not merely hygiene: `dist/networks.js` contains the
# network passphrase, because `networks.ts` is the one file allowed to (06-TEST-PLAN §8). Committing
# build output would put that literal at a path the network-agnostic check does *not* exempt, and the
# check would be correct to reject it. The check itself reads `git ls-files`, so it never sees an
# uncommitted `dist/` either — belt and braces, in opposite directions.
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Python bytecode from the differential reference. Build output, like dist/ and target/.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Property-test snapshots are NOT committed, unlike every other snapshot in the repo.
#
# 06-TEST-PLAN §8 commits snapshots so "a PR that changes behavior shows it in the diff". A
# proptest snapshot cannot serve that: soroban writes one per test *invocation*, proptest invokes
# the body a few hundred times with **randomly generated inputs**, and the file records those
# inputs. Measured 2026-08-19 by running the suite twice on one machine with no code change
# between: 145 files, different content each time (a deposit of 16 060 410 675 became
# 13 944 294 598, and the number of recorded invocations moved). So the gate fails on correct code
# on every run, which is the one failure mode this project has recorded four times and which ends
# with the gate switched off.
#
# The deterministic unit snapshots still commit and still carry the review value. Untracking these
# removes noise, not coverage — the property suite's assertions are what prove I1/I5, and they run
# either way.
contracts/*/test_snapshots/test_properties/

# The generated vector corpus is built on demand, never committed.
#
# It is proptest output, so it differs every run — the same reason
# contracts/*/test_snapshots/test_properties/ is untracked. A corpus is regenerated, not reviewed;
# what gets reviewed is the strategy in test_vectors.rs. Build one with:
#
#   ANTARES_VECTOR_DUMP=$PWD/test-vectors/generated cargo test -p antares-vault generated_vectors
test-vectors/generated/
